    "body": "Walmart ’s multibillion-dollar warehouse automation push is running into packaging problems, robot breakdowns and higher operating costs, according to a recent Wall Street Journal story . The retailer plans to replace shipping boxes that do not fit its automated systems, while some oversized products still require workers to handle them.\nWhat’s Related\nWalmart plans to replace boxes it has used for more than two decades with versions that are 1.5 inches shorter. The new boxes must be taped shut, adding work and expense, although Walmart says they will reduce shelf-stocking time at stores.\nThe transition involves most of Walmart’s roughly 200 U.S. supply chain buildings. Its warehouse operations rely on more than 140,000 workers to unload, sort, store and ship merchandise.\nRob Montgomery, Head of Supply Chain Operations for Walmart U.S., described the transition to the Journal: “We are kind of in this peak complexity.” He also said Walmart is changing its warehouses while keeping them operating, which adds to the difficulty.\nRELATED STORIES : Walmart Ranks Third in Top 25 Supply Chain Rankings | Walmart to Pay Drivers $100 Million in Settlement Over Misleading Pay\nOlder warehouses making things complicated\nMost of Walmart’s supply chain buildings date to the 1980s and ’90s, and about 80% of its automation construction projects are retrofits. Montgomery said Walmart is installing the equipment while keeping warehouses operating, which complicates the transition.\nDifferent products also require different automated systems, often supplied by different companies. Some goods, including large-screen televisions and jumbo bags of dog food, will need manual assistance for the foreseeable future, Montgomery said.\nRepairs and electricity add costs\nSymbotic, the Massachusetts robotics company in which Walmart owns a 12.6% stake, supplies equipment that moves merchandise and builds pallets for shipment to stores. A company spokesman told the Journal that about 5% of its product-moving robots were undergoing repairs at any given time in recent months, down from under 10% last year.\nMonthly electricity bills at Walmart’s automated grocery facilities can reach $800,000 or more during peak heating or cooling months, compared with roughly $250,000 under more manual operations, a person familiar with the situation told the Journal. The engineers needed to run the equipment also cost more than workers doing manual warehouse tasks, the Journal reported.\nDespite those challenges, Walmart continues to expand its use of automation. By August, more than 65% of its stores were receiving some automated freight , and over 50% of its e-commerce inventory was moving through automated systems.\nThe retailer expects spending on store remodeling and supply chain automation to peak this year and next. Montgomery said top executives and the board view automation as a worthwhile long-term project despite the difficulties.\n“We know that if we wait till perfect,” he told the Journal, “it’ll take too long.”\nOther Walmart news\nShipStation Adds Walmart Shipping Rates : ShipStation launched Ship with Walmart, giving U.S. Marketplace sellers access to Walmart -negotiated shipping rates and seller protections within the platform.\nWalmart Plans $1.3 Billion Georgia Fulfillment Center : Walmart plans to build an automated fulfillment center in Carnesville, Georgia, creating 1,000 jobs .\nWalmart Expands Delivery to Dunkin’ Restaurants : Walmart plans to offer delivery from a majority of Dunkin’s 10,000 U.S. restaurants through its app and website.",
